      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2017 John Wiley & Sons Ltd.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "AIM",
            "content": "To investigate the effects of the novel glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide (GIP) analogue, ZP4165, on body weight and glycaemic control in rodents, and to investigate if ZP4165 modulates the anti-obesity and anti-hyperglycaemic effects of a glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) agonist (liraglutide)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "The acute insulinotropic effect of ZP4165 was investigated in rats during an oral glucose tolerance test. The long-term effects of ZP4165 on body weight and glycaemic control, either alone or in combination with liraglutide, were assessed in diet-induced obese mice and diabetic db/db mice."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "ZP4165 showed insulinotropic action in rats. The GIP analogue did not alter the body weight of obese mice but enhanced GLP-1-induced weight loss. In diabetic mice, 4\u2009weeks' dosing with ZP4165 reduced glycated haemoglobin levels vs vehicle by an extent similar to the GLP-1 agonist."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "ZP4165 potentiated the anti-obesity effect of a GLP-1 agonist in obese mice and improved glycaemic control in diabetic mice. These studies support further investigation of dual-incretin therapy as a more effective treatment option than mono GLP-1 medication for type 2 diabetes mellitus and obesity."
          }
        ]
      },
